Answer smgrnblocks from shared memory.

The planner calls smgrnblocks for every table and index it plans
over, on every query. Stock Postgres answers with an lseek so nobody
notices, but under zou the answer lives in the store and each call
became a store round trip, which multiplied every statement by the
store latency, about 2x on the pgbench commit path once the heap and
its index are counted. The chain fence makes this node the single
writer of its relations and every size change flows through the smgr,
so a shared hash kept current by extend, zeroextend, truncate and
unlink is as authoritative as the store itself. A miss reads the
store once per relation fork and seeds without overwriting, since a
concurrent extend already knows a newer size than the store told us.
Create drops any cached entry instead of seeding one, covering redo
creates over existing pages and locator reuse on paths that skip
zouunlink. A full table degrades to uncached rather than erroring.

